```
VectorXd levenbergMarquardt(VectorXd x, VectorXd y, VectorXd p) {
    double lambda = 0.01;
    VectorXd r, p_new;
    MatrixXd J, JT, JJ, H;
    bool stop = false;
    for (int i = 0; i < 100 && !stop; i++) {
        r = y - func(x, p);
        J = jac(x, p);
        JT = J.transpose();
        JJ = JT * J;
        H = JJ + lambda * JJ.diagonal().asDiagonal();
        p_new = p + H.lu().solve(JT * r);
        if ((p - p_new).norm() < TOL1 || r.norm() < TOL2) {
            stop = true;
        }
        p = p_new;
        lambda *= 0.1;
    }
    return p;
}
```
In this version of the function, the loop continues to execute as long as the variable `stop` is false and the iteration count `i` is less than 100. The variable `stop` is set to true if the norm of the difference between the current and new parameters is less than the tolerance TOL1 or the norm of the residual is less than the tolerance TOL2, which causes the loop to exit.

