Here's an updated version of the code with a `delete` method that allows you to remove one or multiple records:
```php
class Entity {
    private $tableName;
    private $fields = array();
    private $records = array();

    public function __construct($tableName) {
        $this->tableName = $tableName;
    }

    public function __get($fieldName) {
        if (!isset($this->fields[$fieldName])) {
            throw new Exception("Field '$fieldName' does not exist in table '$this->tableName'");
        }
        return $this->fields[$fieldName];
    }

    public function __set($fieldName, $value) {
        if (!isset($this->fields[$fieldName])) {
            $this->fields[$fieldName] = array(
                'type' => gettype($value),
                'value' => $value
            );
        } else {
            $this->fields[$fieldName]['value'] = $value;
        }
        $this->records[] = array(
            'field_name' => $fieldName,
            'field_value' => $value
        );
    }

    public function delete($fieldName, $fieldValue) {
        $newRecords = array();
        foreach ($this->records as $record) {
            if ($record['field_name'] != $fieldName || $record['field_value'] != $fieldValue) {
                $newRecords[] = $record;
            }
        }
        $this->records = $newRecords;
    }

    public function save() {
        $serializedData = serialize(array(
            'fields' => $this->fields,
            'records' => $this->records
        ));
        file_put_contents("$this->tableName.data", $serializedData);
    }

    public function load() {
        $serializedData = file_get_contents("$this->tableName.data");
        $data = unserialize($serializedData);
        $this->fields = $data['fields'];
        $this->records = $data['records'];
    }
}
```
You can use the `delete` method to remove a specific record from the table. For example, to delete all records with field `'foo'` and value `'bar'`, you would do:
```perl
$entity->delete('foo', 'bar');
```
Note that this will only remove the record from the in-memory representation of the table, you will need to call `save` to persist the changes to disk.

