If you want to exclude lazy-loaded routes from the main chunk and keep them as separate chunks, you can modify the `SplitChunksPlugin` configuration in `other-app/webpack.config.js`. The key is to avoid merging the chunks related to the lazy-loaded components.

Here's an example of how you can configure `SplitChunksPlugin` for `OtherApp` to exclude lazy-loaded routes from the main chunk:

1. Update your `other-app/webpack.config.js` configuration:
```javascript
const HtmlWebpackPlugin = require('html-webpack-plugin');
const ModuleFederationPlugin = require('webpack/lib/container/ModuleFederationPlugin');

module.exports = {
  // ... other configurations
  plugins: [
    // ... other plugins
    new ModuleFederationPlugin({
      name: 'other_app',
      filename: 'remoteEntry.js',
      exposes: {
        './OtherApp': './src/OtherApp',
      },
      shared: {
        react: { singleton: true },
        'react-dom': { singleton: true },
      },
    }),
    new HtmlWebpackPlugin({
      template: './public/index.html',
    }),
  ],
  optimization: {
    runtimeChunk: false,
    splitChunks: {
      cacheGroups: {
        defaultVendors: {
          test: /[\\/]node_modules[\\/]/,
          priority: -10,
          chunks: 'all',
          name(module, chunks) {
            if (chunks.some((chunk) => chunk.name === 'OtherApp')) {
              return 'main';
            }
            return 'vendors';
          },
          enforce: true,
        },
        default: {
          minChunks: 2,
          priority: -20,
          chunks: 'all',
          reuseExistingChunk: true,
        },
      },
    },
  },
};
```
In this configuration, the `name` property of the `defaultVendors` cache group is changed to a function. This function checks if any of the chunks are named `OtherApp`. If so, it returns `'main'`, which tells Webpack to bundle it into the main chunk. Otherwise, it returns `'vendors'`, which keeps the chunk separate.

With this setup, the main chunk will only include the `OtherApp` component and its dependencies, while the lazy-loaded routes will be kept in separate chunks. These separate chunks will be loaded on-demand when the user navigates to the corresponding routes.

This configuration allows you to maintain the benefits of code splitting and optimizes the loading of `OtherApp` and its lazy-loaded routes.

