You can improve the program to work for any length bitstring by adding a check for the remaining bits at the end of the `$binaryString` and handling them appropriately. Here's an example:
```
$binaryString = "0101010101010101010101010101010101010101010101010101010101010111";
$references = array();

for ($i = 0; $i < strlen($binaryString); $i += 8) {
    $eight_bit_block = substr($binaryString, $i, 8);
    $references[] = &$eight_bit_block;
}

// Check for remaining bits
$remaining_bits = strlen($binaryString) % 8;
if ($remaining_bits > 0) {
    $eight_bit_block = substr($binaryString, -$remaining_bits);
    $references[] = &$eight_bit_block;
}

foreach ($references as &$reference) {
    echo $reference . "\n";
}
```
This version of the program uses the modulo operator `%` to check for the remaining bits at the end of the `$binaryString` and uses the `substr()` function to extract them. If there are any remaining bits, it creates a reference to them and stores it in the `$references` array. This way, the program can handle bitstrings of any length.

Please note that, the last 8bit block may contain trailing zeroes if the lenght of the bitstring is not a multiple of 8. You can handle this by, adding padding at the end of the last 8bit block with `str_pad()` function.

