```php
class SqlRepositoryStorage implements RepositoryStorageInterface {
  private $connection;
  private $tableName;
  private $entityMapping;

  public function __construct(PDO $connection, $tableName, $entityMapping) {
    $this->connection = $connection;
    $this->tableName = $tableName;
    $this->entityMapping = $entityMapping;
  }

  public function getBySpecification(SpecificationInterface $specification): array {
    // Implementation of the getBySpecification method goes here
  }

  public function persist(EntityInterface $entity): void {
    // Implementation of the persist method goes here
  }

  public function commit(): void {
    $query = "INSERT INTO $this->tableName (";
    $values = array();

    // Build the SQL query to insert the new entities
    foreach ($this->entityMapping as $field) {
      $query .= "$field, ";
      $values[":$field"] = $entity->getField($field);
    }
    $query = rtrim($query, ', ') . ") VALUES (";
    foreach ($this->entityMapping as $field) {
      $query .= ":$field, ";
    }
    $query = rtrim($query, ', ') . ")";

    // Prepare the SQL statement and execute it
    $statement = $this->connection->prepare($query);
    $statement->execute($values);
  }
}
```
In this example, the `commit()` method takes the changes that have been made to entities since the last commit, and inserts them into the database using an SQL `INSERT` statement.

Of course, this is just a simple example, and in a real-world scenario, the `commit()` method might need to handle more complex data changes, such as updates and deletions, and might need to use more sophisticated SQL statements to interact with the database.

